			<div class="content" id="post-content-2307858">
									

<p>Now that it makes sense:</p>
<ul>
<li>I <em>love</em> the gonzo concept of an organ that we all have but nobody can see. +1</li>
<li>I don't like that we are just told it is vestigial, and there's no exploration of what an invisible organ is for or how it evolved or why we don't need it. How do we know that it is vestigial? Do other primates have one? On what other organisms is it not vestigial? What happens if you surgically remove it? Can it then be perceived normally? Lots of questions, boring or absent exploration of them. - 1</li>
<li>&quot;What happens if you know about it before you perceive it?&quot; isn't the first question I asked, it probably wouldn't be in the top five. &quot;It kills you&quot; is a boring answer and &quot;redacted&quot; is the most boring way to die. Boring answer to a question that I didn't ask. - 1</li>
</ul>
<p>Recluctantly downvoted. Really this is a sad, well-written waste of a brilliant premise.</p>

